Data-Shared

Path-addressed facade over the 39 Data-*-Shared distributions,
with an API inspired by Data::Path::XS (string, array and compiled
path tiers). A root object holds named mounts; a slash path such as
/cache/42 resolves to a mount plus an intra-structure operation.

    use Data::Shared qw(shared_get shared_set shared_call);
    my $root = Data::Shared->new('/tmp/app.shared');
    $root->mount('cache', 'hashmap:ii', undef, 100_000);
    shared_set($root, '/cache/42', 100);
    shared_get($root, '/cache/42');

See lib/Data/Shared.pm for the full documentation and
lib/Data/Shared/Cookbook.pod for per-dist recipes.

Linux only, 64-bit Perl. Pure Perl; all operations execute in the
underlying XS of each leaf.

Architecture: Data::Shared owns roots, lifecycle coordination, and path dispatch.
Manifest validates/publishes configuration; Storage plans paths and constructs
backends with explicit creation/reopening rules.
Registry declares backend classes and operation mappings; six adapters
translate generic operations to native methods. Compiled paths cache
dispatch and invalidate it when mounts change. Composite mounts keep
a hidden child registry and lazily open one backend per child; Node
provides a relative-path cursor.

Use compiled paths for repeated access and native handles or backend
batch methods for tight loops. File-backed composites bound their
open-child cache; anonymous composites retain children to preserve data.
Configure mount topology in one coordinator before starting workers.
Read-only native access depends on backend support for new_readonly.

Reopening requires every mount backing file to exist; missing child files
also fail when opened lazily. PerfectHash files are built once and then loaded.
Manifest updates are locked and revision checked: reopen a stale root before
retrying a topology change. Failed construction/publication rolls back new
registrations and files. Lifecycle locks coordinate child creation/deletion;
ordinary leaf calls retain backend locking.

For native data-write protection, use
Data::Shared->open_readonly($dir, strict => 1). This rejects unsupported
backends throughout the declared tree. The default retains compatibility
fallbacks. Native minimum versions are recorded in Registry.pm.

Benchmarks: bench/benchmark.pl (flat paths), bench/overhead.pl
(dispatch costs), bench/composite.pl (warm versus evicted children).
Set DS_BENCH_TIME to the minimum CPU seconds per benchmark case.
bench/scaling.pl also measures creation scaling, RSS, cache working sets,
and multiprocess latency; see bench/scaling-results.txt for a recorded run.

Install: perl Makefile.PL && make && make test && make install
